\section{Application: $\Kstar$-Aware Data Filtering}
\label{sec:filtering}

$\Kempirical = 2$ makes a falsifiable prediction: if depth assignments beyond $k{=}2$ carry no discriminative signal, graduated refinement beyond the binary boundary should provide no additional benefit---binary curation should suffice, and graduated performance should converge toward binary as the decay steepness increases.
We test this directly by comparing six data curation strategies on a corpus of 30{,}000 documents (5{,}000 per depth, depths 0--5): (i)~\textbf{no-filter} (uniform weight), (ii)~\textbf{binary-ours} ($w = 1 - P(\text{synthetic})$ from the 15D LightGBM classifier), (iii)~\textbf{binary-Drayson} (importance weighting following \citet{drayson2025}, same classifier, weights $w_j \propto (1 - q(x_j))^b$, $b{=}1$, cap $r_{\max}{=}3$), (iv)~\textbf{graduated} (exponential decay $w = \exp(-\alpha \cdot \hat{d})$, $\alpha \in \{0.1,\ldots,0.9\}$), (v)~\textbf{surprisal} (retain documents above corpus median surprisal), and (vi)~\textbf{DSIR}~\citep{xie2023dsir} (importance resampling against depth-0 reference).

We fine-tune Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct via LoRA (rank~8, $\alpha_{\text{LoRA}}{=}16$) for 3 epochs, evaluating on MMLU (14{,}042 examples, 0-shot) and HellaSwag (10{,}042 examples, 0-shot) across 5 seeds; the graduated strategy is further crossed with 5~$\alpha$ values (51 total runs).

\paragraph{Results.}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Downstream performance of six data filtering strategies. Fine-tuning: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, LoRA, 30K documents, 5 seeds. Graduated shows best $\alpha$ only; full sweep in Table~\ref{tab:tab_alpha_sweep}.}
\label{tab:tab_filtering_main}
\input{tables/tab_filtering_main}
\end{table}

All strategies degrade absolute performance relative to the base model (MMLU 0.6018, HellaSwag 0.6821); the goal is damage minimization, not improvement.
Binary-ours minimizes MMLU degradation most effectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab_filtering_main}), reducing the gap from $-$1.53\,pp (no-filter) to $-$0.77\,pp---an approximately 50\% reduction.
Pairwise paired $t$-tests with Holm-Bonferroni correction confirm that binary-ours significantly outperforms all five alternatives on MMLU ($p_{\text{corr}} < 0.05$); 8 of 15 main-strategy pairs are significant on MMLU, 9 of 15 on HellaSwag (full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:pairwise_significance}).
The strategy ordering is interpretable through $\Kempirical$: binary filtering exploits the one reliable boundary (0v1), while graduated strategies assign different weights to depths 2--5 based on near-random classifier outputs (2v3 $\auc < \theta$), effectively injecting noise into the importance weights.
DSIR performs worst, only marginally above no-filter: its bigram feature hashing captures domain shift but not the depth-correlated distributional signatures that drive depth discrimination.

\paragraph{$\alpha$ sensitivity.}
Higher $\alpha$ values yield monotonically better performance on both benchmarks (HellaSwag: $0.6640$ at $\alpha{=}0.1$ to $0.6701$ at $\alpha{=}0.9$).
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, graduated filtering narrows the MMLU gap to 0.49\,pp below binary-ours ($0.5892$ vs.\ $0.5941$; $p_{\text{corr}} = 0.011$) but matches binary-ours exactly on HellaSwag ($0.6701$; $\Delta = 0$).
The convergence at high $\alpha$ is expected: as $\alpha \to \infty$, graduated weighting degenerates to binary thresholding.
At $\alpha{=}0.9$, depth-2 text receives only 16.5\% weight ($e^{-0.9 \times 2}$), functionally approximating a binary threshold---hence the HellaSwag equivalence, while the more noise-sensitive MMLU still detects the residual difference (detailed $\alpha$ sweep in Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_limitations}).

\paragraph{Summary.}
Binary-ours significantly outperforms all alternatives on MMLU after Holm-Bonferroni correction (8/15 main-strategy pairs significant); on HellaSwag, 9/15 pairs are significant.
On HellaSwag, graduated at $\alpha{=}0.9$ matches binary-ours exactly ($\Delta = 0$, $p_{\text{corr}} = 1.00$), consistent with steep exponential decay functionally approximating binary thresholding; graduated also significantly outperforms surprisal ($p_{\text{corr}} = .002$), while DSIR is indistinguishable from no-filter on both benchmarks.
Significance is assessed within two separate families: the 6 main strategies (15 pairs, addressing ``which strategy is best?'') and the graduated $\alpha$ sweep (``optimal $\alpha$?''), as they correspond to distinct research questions.
The benchmark-specific pattern reinforces $\Kempirical{=}2$: MMLU, which is more sensitive to importance-weight noise, confirms strict dominance of binary filtering; HellaSwag shows that even near-binary graduated weighting suffices, but finer ordinal structure offers no benefit.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate generational depth estimation on four model configurations:
Pythia-1.4B and OLMo-1B in continuation mode, and Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct in both rewrite and continuation modes.
All experiments use the OBD-LightGBM classifier with the 15-dimensional feature set (Section~\ref{sec:method}), with 5{,}000 texts per depth level (generations 0--5).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{$\Kempirical = 2$ Across All Configurations}
\label{sec:kstar_boundary}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ for adjacent depth pairs with bootstrap 95\% CIs and permutation $p$-values for boundary rows.
$\Kempirical = 2$ requires 1v2 $\text{CI}_\text{low} > \theta{=}0.60$ and 2v3 $\text{CI}_\text{high} < \theta$.}
\label{tab:kstar_ci}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-rw} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Pythia} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{OLMo} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-cont} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\cmidrule(lr){8-9}
 & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0v1 & .9956 & & .9932 & & .9970 & & .9971 & \\
1v2 & .6955 & [.686,\,.704]\rlap{$^*$} & .6539 & [.645,\,.663]\rlap{$^*$} & .6145 & [.605,\,.625]\rlap{$^*$} & .6112 & [.602,\,.621]\rlap{$^*$} \\
2v3 & .5893 & [.580,\,.599] & .5514 & [.542,\,.563] & .5496 & [.540,\,.560] & .5609 & [.551,\,.571] \\
3v4 & .5435 & & .5152 & & .5091 & & .5409 & \\
4v5 & .5340 & & .5136 & & .5045 & & .5141 & \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{9}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Permutation $p = 0.002$ for all 1v2 comparisons.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed). The horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show a consistent pattern: 0v1 discrimination is near-perfect ($\auc > 0.99$), followed by a sharp ${\sim}$30\,pp drop at 1v2 ($\auc$: 0.6112--0.6955), with 2v3 falling below $\theta$ (0.5496--0.5893) and deeper pairs converging toward chance.
All four 1v2 CI lower bounds exceed $\theta$ (most marginal: Qwen-continuation at 0.6019), and all four 2v3 CI upper bounds fall below $\theta$, with permutation $p = 0.002$ for all boundary comparisons.
This establishes $\Kempirical = 2$ as a consistent boundary across all tested configurations.
The sharp step-function pattern reflects the concentration of information loss: human text exhibits heavy-tailed, bursty surprisal patterns that are substantially altered by even a single autoregressive pass.
Subsequent generations operate on already-smoothed distributions, yielding progressively smaller marginal changes that fall below detectability within two steps.

The 1v2 $\auc$ varies across models: Qwen-rewrite (0.6955) $>$ Pythia (0.6539) $>$ OLMo (0.6145) $\approx$ Qwen-continuation (0.6112).
The spread due to generation mode within a single model ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp) exceeds the spread across models in the same mode ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp), suggesting generation protocol is a stronger determinant of detectability than model identity.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_kstar_theta.pdf}
\caption{$\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$ across four model-mode configurations, computed using the 95\% CI lower bound criterion (Eq.~\ref{eq:kstar}). The dashed line marks the operational threshold $\theta{=}0.60$ used throughout, where all configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$. Qwen-rewrite yields the highest $\Kempirical$ at all $\theta$; at $\theta{=}0.70$, $\Kempirical{=}1$ universally.}
\label{fig:kstar_theta}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta} shows $\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$ (using 95\% CI lower bounds): at $\theta = 0.55$, $\Kempirical = 3$ for Qwen-rewrite and Qwen-continuation while Pythia and OLMo remain at $\Kempirical = 2$; at $\theta = 0.65$, all continuation modes drop to $\Kempirical = 1$ while rewrite retains $\Kempirical = 2$; at $\theta = 0.70$, $\Kempirical = 1$ universally.
All four decoding configurations (greedy, nucleus, $T{=}0.70$, $T{=}1.2$) likewise yield $\Kempirical = 2$ at $\theta = 0.60$, with 1v2 $\auc$ in $[0.6866, 0.7039]$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap (6-class ordinal accuracy). Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo achieves 90--92\% retention; Qwen-rewrite$\to$continuation-mode models: 61--63\%.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The universality of $\Kempirical = 2$ raises a natural question: does the depth boundary reflect properties of the text itself, or generator-specific artifacts?
Cross-model transfer experiments address this directly.

Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap} shows the transfer accuracy matrix (full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}).
Continuation-mode transfer is high: Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo retains 90--92\% of within-model accuracy, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$Pythia 84--85\%, and Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$OLMo 80--85\%.
At the 0v1 boundary, cross-model $\auc$ exceeds 0.96 for all pairs (range: 0.963--0.991).
These retention rates suggest that depth features transfer across generators when computed by a fixed scorer.
The high retention indicates that the relative importance of depth features is encoded in distributional text properties---surprisal variance, lexical diversity---rather than in generator-specific fingerprints, enabling generator-agnostic deployment when a consistent scorer is used.

Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\% retention to Pythia/OLMo), confirming reliance on mode-specific artifacts.
Cross-mode transfer is asymmetric: continuation$\to$rewrite retains 89\% while rewrite$\to$continuation retains 82\%, indicating that continuation-mode features form a generalizable subset.

\paragraph{Cross-scorer validation.}
To test whether depth signals depend on the scorer model, we replace the Qwen self-scorer with two alternative scorers (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B) for feature extraction on Qwen-generated text.
Feature rankings remain highly correlated across all scorer pairs (Spearman $\rho > 0.90$), confirming that feature ordering is text-intrinsic.
Absolute discriminability, however, is scorer-sensitive at the $\Kempirical$ boundary.
For Qwen-rewrite, $\Kempirical = 2$ is scorer-invariant: 1v2 $\auc$ remains stable under cross-scoring (Pythia: 0.7012, OLMo: 0.6947; self-scorer baseline: 0.6955; Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}).
For Qwen-continuation---already the most marginal self-scorer condition (CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.15\,pp; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci})---$\Kempirical$ drops from 2 to 1 under both alternative scorers (1v2 $\auc$: $0.6112 \to 0.5923$ [Pythia], $0.5858$ [OLMo], both below $\theta$).
This dissociation---scorer-invariant feature rankings but scorer-sensitive boundary classification---indicates that cross-scoring preserves the relative importance structure of depth features while attenuating the absolute signal, with consequences only where the self-scorer margin is already thin.
Crucially, both alternative scorers agree on $\Kempirical$ for each generation mode ($\Kempirical = 2$ for rewrite, $1$ for continuation), indicating that $\Kempirical$ is determined by the generation process rather than the choice of scoring model.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Degradation Ordering}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Top-3 features by single-feature $\auc$ ranking. Bold: shared across all continuation-mode models. Spearman $\rho$ (continuation pairs): Pythia--OLMo 0.95, Pythia--Qwen-cont 0.91, OLMo--Qwen-cont 0.85.}
\label{tab:top_features}
\begin{tabular}{cllll}
\toprule
Rank & Pythia & OLMo & Qwen-rw & Qwen-cont \\
\midrule
1 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \texttt{freq\_entropy} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} \\
2 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} \\
3 & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}} & \textbf{\texttt{surp\_d2\_std}} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Having established that depth signals transfer across models, we next identify which specific features drive this transferable signal.
Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}).
The top-3 features---\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}---are shared across all three families, all measuring surprisal variability.
This shared ordering reveals a common mechanism: autoregressive generation progressively damps surprisal variability.
\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}---the standard deviation of token-to-token surprisal changes---decreases monotonically with depth as the irregular spikes characteristic of human writing are smoothed out, while \texttt{surp\_std} and \texttt{surp\_d2\_std} reflect the narrowing of the overall surprisal distribution.
Qwen-rewrite diverges, with \texttt{freq\_entropy} as its top feature---corroborating the cross-model transfer findings.
A limitation is that depth-pair-level agreement weakens ($\tau \approx 0.37$ at 1v2), indicating per-feature contributions at the boundary vary across models.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Generation Mode}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

The preceding analyses use continuation mode as the default; we now compare generation modes to validate this choice.
Within Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, rewrite mode yields 1v2 $\auc = 0.6955$ versus continuation's $0.6112$ ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp).
The amplified detectability reflects instruction-following artifacts that are discriminable but mode-specific rather than transferable across generators, as confirmed by the poor cross-model transfer of rewrite-mode classifiers (61--63\% vs.\ 80--92\% for continuation).
Continuation mode is therefore recommended as the canonical condition for cross-model comparisons.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Neural Probe}
\label{sec:neural_probe}

A natural question is whether $\Kempirical = 2$ is an artifact of our 15-dimensional feature set, or whether richer representations can push the boundary deeper.
Replacing hand-crafted features with frozen encoder hidden states (MLP probe; Table~\ref{tab:s7_neural_probe}), we evaluate $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$ across all three model families (Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}).
Qwen-continuation achieves 2v3 $\auc = 0.6159$ $[0.610, 0.622]$, yielding $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$.
However, Pythia ($\auc_{2v3} = 0.5963$ $[0.589, 0.603]$) and OLMo ($\auc_{2v3} = 0.5892$ $[0.582, 0.597]$) both fall below $\theta$, giving $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$.
The Qwen-only elevation likely reflects self-familiarity: each model's frozen embeddings probe its own generations, and Qwen's instruction-tuned representations encode mode-specific artifacts that boost boundary discrimination---the same artifacts that transfer poorly across model families (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).
The 15D model-free features yield $\Kempirical = 2$ universally (3/3 models), validating their choice for cross-model deployment.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Neural probe pairwise $\auc$ with bootstrap 95\% CIs (frozen-encoder MLP). $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ for Qwen only; Pythia and OLMo match the 15D boundary at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$.}
\label{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{2v3 $\auc$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B  & 0.6159 & $[0.610,\,0.622]$ & 3 \\
Pythia-1.4B & 0.5963 & $[0.589,\,0.603]$ & 2 \\
OLMo-1B    & 0.5892 & $[0.582,\,0.597]$ & 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

